ts up (pitch up), but the engines stay fixed pointing forward (or slightly downward for some jets). The thrust remains horizontal relative to the plane's body, but since the plane is now tilted, the thrust has a small vertical component. However, that vertical component is tiny—nowhere near enough to lift the plane. The wings provide the overwhelming majority of the upward force. **The sensation you're describing has a name: "seat-of-the-pants" feeling** Pilots call it that because it can be misleading. For example, when a plane turns, you feel pushed into the seat—but that's the centripetal force from the wings tilting, not the engine. Many passengers think the thrust is doing everything, but it's the wings doing the heavy lifting. **Bottom line:** Your feeling that the engine "lifts you up" is a natural misinterpretation of two separate forces (forward thrust and upward lift) happening at the same time. The engine never tilts upward; it stays horizontal. The wings use the speed from that horizontal thrust to create a vertical lift. If you ever get a chance, stand outside near a runway during takeoff. Watch the engines of a commercial jet—they stay pointed exactly the same direction whether the plane is on the ground or climbing. Only the nose rotates. That visual might help your brain separate the two forces.
